    80	\begin{abstract}
    81	Let $K$ be a field with a valuation and let $S$ be the polynomial ring
    82	$S:= K[x_1, \dots, x_n]$.  We discuss the extension of
    83	Gr\"obner theory to ideals in $S$, taking the valuations of
    84	coefficients into account, and describe the Buchberger algorithm in
    85	this context.  In addition we discuss some implementation and
    86	complexity issues.  The main motivation comes from tropical geometry,
    87	as tropical varieties can be defined using these Gr\"obner bases, but we
    88	also give examples showing that the resulting Gr\"obner bases can be
    89	substantially smaller than traditional Gr\"obner bases.  In the case
    90	$K =\mathbb Q$ with the $p$-adic valuation the algorithms have been
    91	implemented in a Macaulay 2 package.
    92	\end{abstract}
    93	
    94	\maketitle
    95	
    96	
    97	\section{Introduction}
    98	
    99	Most work in computational algebraic geometry makes fundamental
   100	use of the theory of Gr\"obner bases.  In this paper we consider
   101	algorithms for a variant of Gr\"obner bases for ideals in a
   102	polynomial ring with coefficients in a valued field.
   103	
   104	A major application for these results is in the rapidly growing field
   105	of tropical geometry.  Let $X$ be the subvariety of $\mathbb P^{n-1}$
   106	defined by a homogeneous ideal $I$ in $S=K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, where $K$
   107	is a field with a valuation $\val \colon K^* \rightarrow \mathbb R$.
   108	Let $X^0$ be the intersection of $X$ with the torus $T \cong
   109	(K^*)^{n-1}$ of $\mathbb P^{n-1}$.  The tropicalization of $X^0$ is
   110	the set of $w \in \mathbb R^n/\mathbb R(1,\dots,1)$ for which the
   111	modified initial ideal $\inn_{w}(I)$ does not contain a monomial.
   112	This has the structure of a polyhedral complex, and many invariants of
   113	$X$ can be recovered from the tropical variety.
   114	
   115	Prior computational work in tropical geometry has focused on 
   116	 ideals with coefficients in either $\mathbb Q$ with the trivial
   117	 valuation or $\overline{\mathbb Q(t)}$, as those cases can be treated
   118	 using standard Gr\"obner techniques.  See the software {\tt
   119	   gfan}~\cite{gfan} for details.  Standard Gr\"obner techniques do
   120	 not suffice for the case of $\mathbb Q$ with the $p$-adic valuation
   121	 $\val_p$, which is of increasing interest thanks to the connections
   122	 between tropical geometry and Berkovich spaces; see, for example,
   123	 \cite{BPR}.
   124	
   125	Section~\ref{s:GrobnerTheory} explains how the standard Gr\"obner
   126	algorithms need to be modified to handle general valued fields $K$,
   127	such as $(\mathbb Q,\val_p)$.  The main issue is that the standard
   128	normal form algorithm need not terminate.  The solution is to replace
   129	it by a modification of Mora's tangent cone algorithm; the main
   130	contribution of this part of the paper is the suggestion of an
   131	appropriate \'ecart function.
   132	Unlike the standard basis case, we get a strong normal form; see
   133	Remark~\ref{r:normalform}.  In Sections~\ref{s:complexity} and
   134	\ref{s:implementation} we discuss complexity and implementation
   135	issues.  Degree bounds are as for usual Gr\"obner bases
   136	(Theorem~\ref{t:degreebound}).  While the valuations of coefficients
   137	in a reduced Gr\"obner basis cannot be bounded by the valuations of
   138	the original generators (Example~\ref{e:unbounded}), for
   139	coefficients in $(\mathbb Q,\val_p)$ we can bound the valuations of
   140	coefficients in a reduced Gr\"obner basis using the valuations and
   141	absolute values of coefficients of the generators; see
   142	Proposition~\ref{proposition.complexity.valbound}.
   143	
   144	A theoretical consequence of these results is a computational proof
   145	that the tropical variety of an ideal only depends on the field
   146	defined by the coefficients of the generators; see
   147	Corollary~\ref{c:tropicaldoesnotdepend}.  We expect these algorithms
   148	to also have applications outside tropical geometry.  In particular,
   149	they can lead to smaller Gr\"obner bases.  In
   150	Section~\ref{s:cardinality} we give a family of ideals in $\mathbb
   151	Q[x_1,x_2,x_3]$ for which the size of the $p$-adic Gr\"obner basis is
   152	constant but the smallest size of a traditional Gr\"obner basis grows
   153	unboundedly.
   154	
   155	  The algorithms have been implemented in a package {\tt
   156	    GrobnerValuations}~\cite{GrobnerValuations} for the computational
   157	  algebraic geometry system {\tt Macaulay2}~\cite{M2}, which is
   158	  available from the authors' webpages.  A preliminary implementation
   159	  is also available in {\tt gfan}~\cite{gfan}.  For a different
   160	  approach to this problem, see \cite{MarkwigRen}.
   161	
   162	\noindent {\bf Acknowledgments.}  We thank Spencer Backman and Anders Jensen for
   163	comments on an earlier draft of this paper.  Maclagan was partially
   164	supported by EPSRC grant EP/I008071/1.
   165	
   166	\section{Gr\"obner Theory}
   167	\label{s:GrobnerTheory}
   168	
   169	In this section we generalize the standard Buchberger algorithm for
   170	Gr\"obner bases so that it takes the valuations of coefficients
   171	into account.  The key algorithm is Algorithm~\ref{a:normalForm},
   172	which computes the normal form of a polynomial.
   173	
   174	Let $K$ be a field with a valuation $\val \colon K^* \rightarrow
   175	\mathbb R$.  We denote by $R := \{ a \in K : \val(a) \geq 0 \} \cup
   176	\{0\}$ the valuation ring of $K$, by $\mathfrak{m}:= \{ a \in K :
   177	\val(a) >0 \} \cup \{0 \}$ the maximal ideal of the local ring $R$,
   178	and by $\K := R/\mathfrak{m}$ the residue field.  For $a \in R$ we
   179	denote by $\overline{a}$ the image of $a$ in $\K$.  The image of the
   180	valuation map is denoted by $\Gamma$, and is an additive subgroup of
   181	$\mathbb R$.  We assume that there exists a group homomorphism $\phi :
   182	\Gamma \rightarrow K^*$ with $\val(\phi(w))= w$.  This always exists
   183	if $K$ is algebraically closed (see \cite[Lemma
   184	  2.1.15]{TropicalBook}).  For example, the field $K=\mathbb Q(t)$ has
   185	valuation $\val(f/g) = a$ when the Taylor series for $f/g$ is $\alpha
   186	t^a + $ higher order terms.  In this case we can set $\phi(w)=t^w$.
   187	We use the notation $w \mapsto t^w$ for the homomorphism $\phi$ for an
   188	arbitrary field.  We make frequent use of the $p$-adic valuation
   189	$\val_p$ on $\mathbb Q$.  If $a =p^mb/c$, where $p$ does not divide
   190	$b$ or $c$ then $\val_p(a)=m$.  In this case $\Gamma = \mathbb Z$, and
   191	we can take $\phi$ to be $\phi(w)=p^w$.  Another standard, though less
   192	computationally effective, choice of field is the Puiseux series
   193	$\PuiseuxC$ with valuation the lowest exponent occurring.
   194	
   195	Let $S$ be the polynomial ring $K[x_1, \dots, x_n]$, and fix a weight
   196	vector $w \in \mathbb R^n$.  For $f = \sum_{u \in \mathbb N^n} c_u x^u
   197	\in S$, let $W:=\trop(f)(w)=\min ( \val(c_u)+ w \cdot u : c_u \neq
   198	0)$.  The initial term of $f$ with respect to $w$ is $$\inn_{w}(f) =
   199	\sum_{\val(c_u)+w \cdot u = W} \overline{t^{-\val(c_u)}c_u} x^u \in
   200	\K[x_1,\dots,x_n].$$ The initial ideal of a homogeneous ideal $I
   201	\subset S$ with respect to $w \in \mathbb R^n$ is $$\inn_{w}(I) = \langle
   202	\inn_{w}(f) : f \in I \rangle \subseteq \K[x_1,\dots,x_n].$$ Note that
   203	$\inn_{w}(I)$ is an ideal in $\K[x_1,\dots, x_n]$.  A finite set
   204	$\mathcal G = \{g_1,\dots, g_s \} \subset I$ is called a {\em
   205	  Gr\"obner basis} for $I$ with respect to $w$ if $\inn_{w}(I) =
   206	\langle \inn_{w}(g_1) , \dots , \inn_{w}(g_s) \rangle$.  The
   207	requirement that the ideal $I$ be homogeneous is not necessary to
   208	define an initial ideal, but is for a Gr\"obner basis to have expected
   209	properties; see Remark~\ref{r:homogeneity}.
   210	
   211	This modification of the original definition of a Gr\"obner basis
   212	comes from tropical geometry; see \cite{SpeyerThesis}.  When the
   213	valuation on $K$ is trivial this initial ideal is the standard initial
   214	ideal of $I$ with respect to the weight vector $-w$.  While many
   215	properties of these Gr\"obner bases are well understood when the
   216	valuation is nontrivial, computational issues have not yet been
   217	addressed in the literature.
   218	
   219	\begin{example}
   220	Let $f= 3x^2+xy+18y^2 \in \mathbb Q[x,y]$, where $\mathbb Q$ has the
   221	$3$-adic valuation.  For $w = (0,0)$ we have $W = 0$, and $\inn_w(f) =
   222	xy \in \mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z[x,y]$.  For $w=(1,4)$ we have $W=3$, and
   223	$\inn_w(f) = x^2$, and for $w=(2,0)$ we have $W=2$ and $\inn_w(f) =
   224	xy+\overline{3^{-2}18}y^2 = xy+2y^2$.
   225	\end{example}
   226	
   227	A Gr\"obner basis for an ideal $I$ can be computed by a modification
   228	of the standard Buchberger algorithm 
   229	as we explain below.  The main difference is in the normal form algorithm
   230	for the remainder of a polynomial on division by a set of other
   231	polynomials.  The difficulty is that a naive implementation of the
   232	normal form algorithm need not terminate, as the following example
   233	shows.
   234	
   235	
   236	\begin{example} \label{e:nontermination}
   237	Let $K=\mathbb{Q}$ with the $2$-adic valuation.  Consider the standard
   238	normal form algorithm, where the term to be canceled at each stage is
   239	taken to be the term with the lowest valuation of the coefficient.  
   240	Using this to compute the remainder of $x \in \mathbb Q[x,y,z]$ on
   241	division by $\{x-2y,y-2z,z-2x\}$, we reduce $x$ by $x-2y$ to get $2y$.
   242	This is then reduced by $y-2z$ to get $4z$, which in turn is reduced
   243	by $z-2x$ to get $8x$.  This reduction continues indefinitely.
   244	\end{example}
   245	
   246	This problem also arises in the theory of standard bases; see for
   247	example \cite[\S 4.3]{CLO2}.  The solution in that setting, Mora's
   248	tangent cone algorithm, is to allow division by previous partial
   249	quotients.  Termination is assured by a descending nonnegative integer
   250	invariant called the \'ecart which measures the difference in degrees
   251	between two possible initial terms of a polynomial.  A difficulty in
   252	generalizing this function to Gr\"obner bases with valuations is that
   253	this difference must take the valuations of the coefficients into
   254	account, so would naturally lie in the not-necessarily-well-ordered
   255	group $\Gamma$.  Even for the valuation $\val_p$ on $\mathbb Q$, where
   256	$\Gamma = \mathbb Z$, the standard \'ecart function does not work
   257	directly.
   258	
   259	The following algorithm modifies Mora's algorithm to take into account
   260	the valuations of the coefficients.  It uses a function $E(f,g)$,
   261	which takes two homogeneous polynomials and returns a nonnegative
   262	integer.  In Lemma~\ref{l:normalForm.terminates} we give one option
   263	for this function that ensures termination.  We present the algorithm
   264	with the function $E$ unspecified as more efficient functions $E$ may
   265	exist.
   266	
   267	As in all normal form algorithms this is a generalization of long
   268	division, which works by canceling the ``leading term'' of the
   269	polynomial $f$.  An added complication is that we do not assume that
   270	the weight vector $w$ is generic, so the leading term $\inn_w(f)$ is
   271	not necessarily a monomial.  For this reason we also fix an arbitrary
   272	monomial term order $\prec$ (in the sense of usual Gr\"obner theory)
   273	to determine which term of $\inn_w(f)$ to cancel.  If $w$ is
   274	sufficiently generic with respect to the input polynomials $\prec$
   275	will play no role.  For $f \in K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, 
   276	$\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(f)) = \alpha x^u$ denotes the leading term, including the
   277	coefficient.  We denote by $\lm(f)$ the monomial $x^u$
   278	occurring in $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(f))$, and by $\lc(f)$ the
   279	coefficient of $x^u$ in $f$.  Note that $\lc(f) \in K$, not
   280	$\K$, and that $\lc(f)$ and $\lm(f)$ depend on both $w$ and $\prec$.
   281	
   282	We also use the following partial order on polynomials, which plays
   283	the role of comparing initial monomials in usual Gr\"obner bases.
   284	
   285	\begin{definition} \label{d:totalorder}
   286	Fix homogeneous polynomials $f,g \in K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, $w \in \mathbb
   287	R^n$, and a term order $\prec$.  Write $\lm(f)=x^u$, $\lm(g)=x^v$,
   288	$\lc(f)=a$, and $\lc(g)=b$.  Then $f < g$ if $\val(a)+w \cdot u <
   289	\val(b) + w \cdot v$ or $\val(a)+ w \cdot u = \val(b) + w \cdot v$ and
   290	$x^u \succ x^v$.  In addition we set $f < 0$ for all nonzero $f$.
   291	This is consistent with the first part of the definition if we regard
   292	the valuation as a function $\val \colon K \rightarrow \mathbb R \cup
   293	\{ \infty \}$.
   294	\end{definition}
   295	
   296	For example, if $\mathbb Q$ has the $2$-adic valuation, $w=(1,2)$ and
   297	$\prec$ is the lexicographic term order with $x_1 \succ x_2$, then $x_1^2
   298	< x_2^2 < x_1^5 < 2x_2^2$.  Note that if $f \geq h$ and
   299	$g \geq h$ then $f \pm g \geq h$.
   300	
   301	\begin{algorithm} \label{a:normalForm} 
   302	
   303	\noindent {\bf Input: } Homogeneous polynomials $\{g_1,\dots, g_s \}$, a homogeneous polynomial $f$ in
   304	$S=K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, a weight vector $w\in\mathbb R^n$, and a
   305	term order $\prec$.
   306	
   307	\noindent {\bf Output: } Homogeneous polynomials $h_1,\dots,h_s, r \in S$ 
   308	satisfying
   309	$$f=\sum_{i=1}^s h_i g_i +r,$$
   310	where 
   311	$h_ig_i \geq f$ for $1 \leq i \leq s$,
   312	and $r \geq f$.
   313	Write $r=\sum b_v x^v$ with $b_v \in K$.  Then in addition $b_v \neq 0$
   314	implies $x^v$ is not divisible by any $\lm(g_i)$.
   315	
   316	We call $r$ a \emph{remainder}, or \emph{normal form}, of dividing $f$
   317	by $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$.
   318	
   319	
   320	\begin{enumerate}
   321	
   322	\item {\bf Initialize: } Set $T = \{ g_1,\dots,g_s \},
   323	  h_{10}=\dots=h_{s0}=0, q_0 =f, r_0=0$.  Set $j=0$.
   324	
   325	\item {\bf Loop: } While $q_j \neq 0$ do:
   326	
   327	\begin{enumerate}
   328	\item {\bf Move to remainder: } If there is no $g \in T$ with
   329	  $\lm(g)$ dividing $\lm(q_j)$, then set
   330	  $r_{j+1}=r_j+\lc(q_j)\lm(q_j)$, $q_{j+1}=q_j -
   331	  \lc(q_j)\lm(q_j)$, and $h_{i{j+1}}=h_{ij}$ for all
   332	  $i$.  Set $T = T \cup \{q_j \}$.
   333	
   334	\item {\bf Divide: } Otherwise:  \label{i:divide}
   335	\begin{enumerate}
   336	\item \label{i:Chooseg} Choose $g \in T$ such that $\lm(g)$ divides
   337	  $\lm(q_j)$ with $E(q_j,g)$ minimal among all such choices.  
   338	\item If
   339	$E(q_j,g) >0$ then set $T=T \cup \{q_j\}$. 
   340	
   341	\item Since $\lm(g)$ divides $\lm(q_j)$ there is a
   342	  monomial $x^v$ with $\lm(x^vg)=\lm(q_j)$.  Set
   343	  $c_v = \lc(q_j)/\lc(x^vg) \in K$.  Let
   344	  $p=q_j-c_vx^vg$.
   345	
   346	\item 
   347	If $g = g_{m}$ for some $1 \leq m \leq s$, then set $q_{j+1}=p$,
   348	$h_{m{j+1}}=h_{mj}+c_vx^v$, $h_{i{j+1}}=h_{ij}$ for
   349	$i \neq m$, and $r_{j+1}=r_j$.
   350	
   351	\item  \label{i:v} If $g$ was added to $T$ at some previous iteration of the
   352	  algorithm, so $g=q_m$ for some $m<j$, then set $q_{j+1}=1/(1-c_v)p$,
   353	 $h_{i{j+1}}=1/(1-c_v)(h_{ij}-c_vh_{im})$, and $r_{j+1}=
   354	1/(1-c_v)(r_j-c_vr_m)$.
   355	\end{enumerate}
   356	
   357	\item $j=j+1$.
   358	
   359	
   360	\end{enumerate}
   361	
   362	\item {\bf Output: } Output $h_i=h_{ij}$ for $1 \leq i \leq s$, and $r=r_j$.
   363	
   364	\end{enumerate}
   365	
   366	\end{algorithm}
   367	
   368	
   369	\begin{example}
   370	Let $f = x^2+y^2+z^2 \in \mathbb Q[x,y,z]$ where $\mathbb Q$ has the
   371	$2$-adic valuation, and let $g_1=y+16z$.  Fix $w = (3,2,1)$, and let
   372	$\prec$ be the lexicographic order with $x \prec y \prec z$.  For
   373	clarity we underline the term of a polynomial $f$ containing
   374	$\lm(f)$.  We do not specify the function $E(f,g)$, assuming
   375	that it is always positive.  Then the algorithm proceeds as follows.
   376	
   377	\begin{enumerate}
   378	
   379	\item $T=\{\underline{y}+16z\}$, $h_{10}=0$,
   380	$q_0=x^2+y^2+\underline{z^2}$, $r_0=0$, $j=0$.
   381	
   382	\item $T=\{\underline{y}+16z, x^2+y^2+\underline{z}^2\}$, $h_{11}=0$,
   383	$q_1=x^2+\underline{y}^2$, $r_1=z^2$, $j=1$.
   384	
   385	\item $T=\{\underline{y}+16z, x^2+y^2+\underline{z}^2,
   386	x^2+\underline{y}^2 \}$, $h_{12}=y$, $q_2=\underline{x}^2-16yz$,
   387	$r_2=z^2$, $j=2$.
   388	
   389	\item $T = \{\underline{y}+16z, x^2+y^2+\underline{z}^2,
   390	x^2+\underline{y}^2, \underline{x}^2-16yz \}$, $h_{13}=y$,
   391	$q_3=-16yz$, $r_3=x^2+z^2$, $j=3$.
   392	
   393	\item $T= \{\underline{y}+16z,x^2+y^2+\underline{z}^2,
   394	x^2+\underline{y}^2, \underline{x}^2-16yz, -16yz \}$,
   395	  $h_{14}=y-16z$, $q_4 = 256z^2$, $r_4=x^2+z^2$, $j=4$.
   396	
   397	
   398	\item $T= \{\underline{y}+16z,x^2+y^2+\underline{z}^2,
   399	x^2+\underline{y}^2, \underline{x}^2-16yz, -16yz , 256z^2 \}$.  In
   400	this case we divide by $g=x^2+y^2+z^2=q_0$, so $c_v=256$.  Thus
   401	$h_{15}=-1/255(y-16z)$, $q_5=1/255(256x^2+\underline{256y}^2)$,
   402	$r_5=-1/255(x^2+z^2)$, and $j=5$.
   403	
   404	\item $T= \{\underline{y}+16z,x^2+y^2+\underline{z}^2,
   405	x^2+\underline{y}^2, \underline{x}^2-16yz, -16yz , -16z^2, 256/255x^2
   406	+ \underline{255/256y}^2 \}$.  Then $g=x^2+y^2=q_1$, so $c_v=256/255$.
   407	 Thus $h_{16}=255(1/255(y-16z))=y-16z$, $q_6=0$,
   408	$r_6=-255(-1/255(x^2+z^2) - 256/255z^2)=x^2+257z^2$, and $j=6$.
   409	
   410	\item Output $h_1=y-16z$ and $r=x^2+257z^2$.
   411	\end{enumerate}
   412	
   413	Note that $x^2+y^2+z^2 = (y-16z)(y+16z)+x^2+257z^2$ and no term of
   414	$x^2+257z^2$ is divisible by $\lm(y+16z)=y$.
   415	
   416	\end{example}
   417	
   418	
   419	\begin{proof}[Proof of correctness]
   420	
   421	We show correctness assuming termination.  
   422	
   423	We show that the following properties hold at each stage of the algorithm:
   424	\begin{enumerate}
   425	\item  $f= q_j + \sum_{i=1}^s h_{ij}g_i +r_j$;   \label{enum:equality}
   426	\item $h_{ij}g_i \geq f$; \label{enum:larger} 
   427	\item $r_j \geq f$; \label{enum:order1}
   428	\item No term of $r_j$ is divisible by any $\lm(g_i)$; \label{enum:remainder}
   429	\item  $q_j \geq f$; \label{enum:order2}
   430	\item If $q_{j+1} \neq 0$ then $q_{j+1} >
   431	  q_{j}$.  \label{enum:valuationgrowing}
   432	\end{enumerate}
   433	 These properties all hold at the initialization step by construction.
   434	 We now show they continue to hold after each of the three types of
   435	 iteration step.  We also show that in step \ref{i:v} of the algorithm
   436	 we have $1-c_v \neq 0$.  In all cases, write
   437	 $\lc(q_j)\lm(q_j) = c_jx^{\alpha_j}$.  There are three
   438	 possibilities for the division step, which we consider separately.
   439	
   440	{\bf Case 1:} {\em Move to remainder}.  Suppose there is no $g \in T$
   441	with $\lm(g)$ dividing $\lm(q_j)$.  Then the only values that change
   442	are $q_j$ and $r_j$, but we have $q_j+r_j=q_{j+1}+r_{j+1}$ by
   443	construction, so the equality \ref{enum:equality} holds.
   444	Condition~\ref{enum:larger} holds at stage $j+1$ since it held at
   445	stage $j$.  Since properties~\ref{enum:order1} and~\ref{enum:order2}
   446	hold for $j$, property~\ref{enum:order1} holds for $j+1$.  The term
   447	that is added to $r_{j+1}$ is not divisible by any $\lm(g_i)$, so
   448	property~\ref{enum:remainder} still holds.  The term
   449	$c_{j+1}x^{\alpha_{j+1}}$ is a nonleading term of $q_j$, so
   450	property~\ref{enum:valuationgrowing} follows, which also implies
   451	property~\ref{enum:order2}.
   452	
   453	{\bf Case 2:} {\em Divide, with $g =g_m$}.  Suppose the chosen $g$
   454	with $\lm(g)$ dividing $\lm(q_j)$ is $g_{m}$ for some $1 \leq m \leq
   455	s$.  Since $q_j+h_{mj}g_{m} = q_{j+1}+h_{mj+1}g_m$ by construction,
   456	the equality~\ref{enum:equality} holds in this case as well.  Since
   457	$h_{mj}g_m \geq f$, and $q_j \geq f$, we have $h_{mj+1}g_m
   458	\geq f$.  As the remainder term does not change
   459	properties~\ref{enum:order1} and~\ref{enum:remainder} still hold.
   460	Since $q_{j+1}=q_j-c_vx^vg_m$, we cancel the leading term of $q_j$, so
   461	all terms of $q_{j+1}$ are the sum of a nonleading term of $q_j$ and a
   462	term of $c_vx^vq_m$ that is larger than $c_jx^{\alpha_j}$.  This
   463	implies that $q_j < q_{j+1}$
   464	(property~\ref{enum:valuationgrowing}), which implies
   465	property~\ref{enum:order2} for $j+1$ as above.
   466	
   467	{\bf Case 3: } {\em Divide, with $g = q_m$}.  Finally, we consider the
   468	case that the chosen $g$ with $\lm(g)$ dividing $\lm(q_j)$ is $q_m$
   469	for some $m<j$.  Since all $q_i$ are homogeneous of the same degree,
   470	$x^v=1$ in this setting, and $c_v=c_j/c_m$.  Since
   471	property~\ref{enum:valuationgrowing} holds for all smaller values, we
   472	have $\val(c_j)+w \cdot \alpha_j > \val(c_m)+w \cdot \alpha_m$.  Thus
   473	$x^{\alpha_m}=x^{\alpha_j}$ implies $\val(c_v)>0$, so $1-c_v \neq 0$.
   474	
   475	Now $f=q_m +\sum_{i=1}^s h_{im}g_i + r_m$, so $q_{j+1}=
   476	1/(1-c_v)(q_j-c_vq_m)$, which equals $1/(1-c_v)((f-\sum_{i=1}^s
   477	h_{ij}g_i -r_j) - c_v(f-\sum_{i=1}^s h_{im}g_i-r_m))$.  Thus $f =
   478	q_{j+1} + \sum_{i=1}^s 1/(1-c_v)(h_{ij}-c_vh_{im}) g_i +
   479	1/(1-c_v)(r_j-c_vr_m) = q_{j+1} + \sum_{i=1}^s h_{ij+1} g_i +r_{j+1}$.
   480	This is equality~\ref{enum:equality}.
   481	
   482	Since $\val(1-c_v)=0$, we have $\val(1/(1-c_v))=0$.  Note the
   483	following property of the order $<$ of
   484	Definition~\ref{d:totalorder}: if $p_1 \geq p_2$ and $c \in K$
   485	satisfies $\val(c) \geq 0$ then $cp_1 \geq p_2$.  Then
   486	properties~\ref{enum:larger} and \ref{enum:order1} for $j+1$ follow
   487	from the analogous properties for $j$ and $m$.  No term in either
   488	$r_j$ or $r_m$ is divisible by any $\lm(g_i)$, so the
   489	same is true for $r_{j+1}$.  Finally $p > q_j$ by construction, so
   490	$q_{j+1} = 1/(1-c_v)p > q_j$ as above, so
   491	properties~\ref{enum:order2} and~\ref{enum:valuationgrowing} also
   492	hold.
   493	\end{proof}
   494	
   495	\begin{lemma}\label{l:normalForm.terminates}
   496	For homogeneous polynomials $f, g \in S$ with $f = \sum c_u x^u$ and
   497	$g = \sum b_u x^u$, set $E(f,g) := |\{ u : b_u \neq 0, c_u = 0 \}|$.
   498	Algorithm~\ref{a:normalForm} terminates for this choice of function $E$.  
   499	\end{lemma}
   500	
   501	\begin{proof}
   502	There are only a finite number of possible supports $\supp(q_j) = \{u
   503	: c_u \neq 0 \}$ of the polynomials $q_j = \sum c_u x^u$, as they all
   504	have the same degree.  Thus after some step $j$ no new support will
   505	occur, so there will be $q_m \in T_j$ with $\supp(q_m) \subseteq
   506	\supp(q_j)$, and so $E(q_j,q_m)=0$.  Since we remove the leading term
   507	of $q_j$ at the $j$th step, either by moving it to the remainder, or
   508	by canceling it, when $\supp(q_m) \subseteq \supp(q_j)$ we have
   509	$\supp(q_{j+1}) \subsetneq \supp(q_j)$.  Since the size of the support
   510	cannot decrease indefinitely, the algorithm must terminate.
   511	\end{proof}
   512	
   513	
   514	\begin{remark} \label{r:normalform}
   515	Note that Algorithm~\ref{a:normalForm} gives a strong normal form (no
   516	term of the remainder is divisible by any of the monomials $\{
   517	\lm(g_i) : 1 \leq i \leq s \}$), as opposed to the weak normal form
   518	that occurs in the standard basis case.  This is a consequence of
   519	restricting to homogeneous input; see Remark~\ref{r:homogeneity} for more on
   520	this topic.  See \cite[\S 1.6]{singularbook} for details of normal
   521	forms in the standard basis case.
   522	\end{remark}
   523	
   524	\begin{remark} \label{r:Zmodpm}
   525	Algorithm~\ref{a:normalForm} also holds, with the same proof in the
   526	following modified setting.  Let $K=\mathbb Q$ with the $p$-adic
   527	valuation.  The valuation $\val_p$ restricts to a function, which we
   528	also denote by $\val_p$, from $\mathbb Z/p^m\mathbb Z$ to the
   529	semigroup $\{0,1,\dots,m-1 \} \cup \{\infty\}$, where $\infty$ acts as an
   530	absorbing element.  Note that $\val_p(ab) = \val_p(a)+\val_p(b)$ and
   531	$\val_p(a+b) \geq \min(\val_p(a),\val_p(b))$ for $a,b \in \mathbb
   532	Z/p^m \mathbb Z$.  We can then define the partial order $<$ on
   533	polynomials in $\mathbb Z/p^m\mathbb Z[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ in the same way
   534	as in Definition~\ref{d:totalorder}.  Also note that in step \ref{i:v}
   535	of the algorithm, since $1-c_v$ has valuation zero (as shown in the
   536	proof), it is not divisible by $p$, so is a unit in $\mathbb
   537	Z/p^m\mathbb Z$.  This means that the algorithm and its proof go
   538	through in this setting.  This variant is used in
   539	Section~\ref{ss:Zmodpm}.
   540	\end{remark}
   541	
   542	As in the usual Gr\"obner setting, we can use the normal form
   543	algorithm to compute a Gr\"obner basis using the Buchberger algorithm.
   544	Let $f, g$ be two polynomials in $K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$. 
   545	  We define the
   546	$S$-polynomial of $f$ and $g$ to be
   547	\begin{equation*}
   548	S(f,g):=\lc(g) \frac{\lcm(\lm(f),\lm(g))}{\lm(f)}f-\lc(f)\frac{\lcm(\lm(f),\lm(g))}{\lm(g)}g.
   549	\end{equation*}
   550	
   551	
   552	\begin{algorithm}\label{a:Buchberger}
   553	
   554	\noindent {\bf Input: } A list $\{ f_1, \dots, f_l \}$ of homogeneous
   555	polynomials in $S$, a weight-vector $w \in \mathbb R^n$, and a term order $\prec$.
   556	
   557	\noindent {\bf Output: } A list $\{g_1, \dots, g_s \}$ of homogeneous
   558	polynomials in $I= \langle f_1,\dots,f_l \rangle$ such that $\{
   559	\inn_\prec(\inn_{w}(g_i)) : 1 \leq i \leq s\}$ generates
   560	$\inn_\prec(\inn_{w}(I))$.
   561	
   562	\begin{enumerate}
   563	\item Set $\mathcal G = \{ f_1,\dots, f_l \}$.  Set $\mathcal P = \{
   564	(g, g') : g, g' \in \mathcal G \}$.
   565	\item While $\mathcal P \neq \emptyset$:
   566	 \begin{enumerate}
   567	\item Pick $(g,g') \in \mathcal P$.
   568	\item Let $r$ be the normal form on  dividing $S(g,g')$ by $\mathcal G$.
   569	If $r \neq 0$ then set $\mathcal G = \mathcal G \cup \{r\}$, and
   570	$\mathcal P = \mathcal P \cup \{ (r, g) : g \in \mathcal G \}$.
   571	\end{enumerate}
   572	\item Return $\mathcal G$.
   573	\end{enumerate}
   574	\end{algorithm}
   575	
   576	
   577	The proof of the termination and correctness of this algorithm is
   578	almost exactly the same as the proof for usual Gr\"{o}bner bases,
   579	which can be found for example in~\cite{CLO}.  We indicate below the
   580	necessary changes, which use the following lemma.
   581	
   582	\begin{lemma} \label{l:vectors}
   583	Fix $v_1,\dots,v_m \in K^n$, and $\beta_1,\dots,\beta_m \in \mathbb R$.  For
   584	$\lambda \in K^m$, write $s(\lambda) = \min(\val(\lambda_i)+\beta_i)$.
   585	Then for fixed $v \in \spann(v_1,\dots,v_m)$ there is a choice of
   586	$\lambda \in K^m$ with $\sum \lambda_i v_i = v$ that maximizes
   587	$s(\lambda)$ among all such choices.
   588	\end{lemma}
   589	\begin{proof}
   590	We first show that for any $\lambda$ with $\sum \lambda_i v_i = v$
   591	there is a $\lambda'$ with $\sum \lambda'_i v_i = v$, $\{ v_i:
   592	\lambda'_i \neq 0 \}$ linearly independent, and $s(\lambda') \geq
   593	s(\lambda)$.  Indeed, if $\{ v_i : \lambda_i \neq 0 \}$ is linearly
   594	dependent, then there is a nonzero $c \in K^m$ with $\sum c_i v_i = 0$
   595	and $c_i \neq 0$ only when $\lambda_i \neq 0$.  After relabeling we
   596	may assume that $\val(c_1)+\beta_1 = \min(\val(c_i)+\beta_i)$.  Since
   597	this then implies that $\val(c_1) \neq \infty$, we have $c_1 \neq 0$,
   598	so we may rescale so that $c_1 =\lambda_1$.  Let
   599	$\lambda'=\lambda-c$.  Then for every $j$
   600	\begin{align*}
   601	\val(\lambda'_j)+\beta_j & = \val(\lambda_j-c_j)+\beta_j\\ & \geq
   602	\min(\val(\lambda_j),\val(c_j))+\beta_j \\ & = \min(\val(\lambda_j)+\beta_j,
   603	\val(c_j)+\beta_j)\\ & \geq \min(\val(\lambda_j)+\beta_j,
   604	\val(\lambda_1)+\beta_1)\\ & \geq s(\lambda),
   605	\end{align*}
   606	 so $s(\lambda') \geq s(\lambda)$.  Since $\{ i: \lambda'_i \neq 0 \}
   607	 \subsetneq \{ i: \lambda_i \neq 0\}$, after iterating a finite number
   608	 of times $\{v_i : \lambda'_i \neq 0 \}$ is linearly independent.  The
   609	 lemma then follows from the observation that if $\{ v_i : \lambda_i
   610	 \neq 0\}$ is linearly independent, then the $\lambda_i$ are
   611	 determined, so the maximum $s(\lambda)$ is achieved at one of these
   612	 finitely many choices.
   613	\end{proof}
   614	
   615	\begin{proof}[Proof of termination and correctness of Algorithm~\ref{a:Buchberger}]
   616	Since at each stage the ideal $\langle \inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g)) : g \in
   617	\mathcal G \rangle$ strictly increases, termination follows as in the
   618	standard case from the fact that the polynomial ring is Noetherian.
   619	
   620	The proof of correctness is also essentially the same as in the
   621	standard case; we include it as it takes essentially the same amount
   622	of space as indicating the changes.  Suppose at the end of the
   623	algorithm $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(f)) \not \in \langle
   624	\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_i)) : 1 \leq i \leq s \rangle$ for some
   625	homogeneous $f \in I$.  Since the $f_i$ are contained in $\mathcal G$,
   626	we can write $f = \sum h_i g_i$ for some homogeneous polynomials
   627	$h_i$.  Write $\lm(h_ig_i) = x^{u_i}$.  We may assume that
   628	$\min(\val(\lc(h_ig_i))+ w \cdot u_i)$ is maximal over all choices of
   629	counterexample $f$ and description $f=\sum h_i g_i$.  That a maximum
   630	exists follows from Lemma~\ref{l:vectors} applied to the vector space
   631	$S_{\deg(f)}$, with the $v_i$ all polynomials of the form $x^{u}g_j$
   632	where $x^u$ is a monomial of degree $\deg(f)-\deg(g_j)$, and $\beta_i
   633	= w \cdot u'$ for $\lm(x^ug_j)=x^{u'}$.  After renumbering we may
   634	assume that $\min(\val(\lc(h_ig_i))+w \cdot u_i) = \val(\lc(h_jg_j))+w
   635	\cdot u_j$ for $1 \leq j \leq d$, and that in addition
   636	$x^{u_1}=x^{u_i}$ for $1 \leq i \leq d' \leq d$ with $x^{u_1}$ the
   637	largest $x^{u_i}$ among those $i \leq d$.  We may further assume that
   638	$d'$ is as small as possible among descriptions achieving the maximum.
   639	Since
   640	$\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(h_ig_i))=\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(h_i))\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_i))
   641	\in \langle \inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_1)),\dots,\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_s))
   642	\rangle$, $x^{u_1} \neq \lm(f)$.  This means that $\lm(\sum_{i=1}^{d'}
   643	h_ig_i) \neq \lm(f)$, so $\val(\sum_{i=1}^{d'} \lc(h_ig_i)) \geq
   644	\min(\val(\lc(h_ig_i)))$, and so in particular $d' \geq 2$.  By
   645	hypothesis we can write $S(g_1,g_2) = \sum_{i=1}^s h'_i g_i$ with
   646	$h'_ig_i \geq S(g_1,g_2)$.  Then
   647	\begin{align*} 
   648	f & = \sum_{i=1}^{s} h_ig_i\\
   649	& = \sum_{i=1}^{s} h_ig_i -
   650	  \frac{\lc(h_1g_1)x^{u_1}}{\lc(g_1)\lc(g_2)\lcm(\lm(g_1),\lm(g_2))}(S(g_1,g_2)
   651	  - \sum_{i=1}^s h'_ig_i)\\
   652	& = (h_1 -  \frac{\lc(h_1g_1)x^{u_1}}{\lc(g_1)\lm(g_1)}+
   653	\frac{\lc(h_1g_2)x^{u_1}}{\lc(g_1)\lc(g_2)\lcm(\lm(g_1),\lm(g_2))}
   654	h'_1)g_1 + \\
   655	& \hspace{1cm} (h_2-\frac{\lc(h_1g_1)x^{u_1}}{\lc(g_1)\lm(g_2)}+
   656	\frac{\lc(h_1g_2)x^{u_1}}{\lc(g_1)\lc(g_2)\lcm(\lm(g_1),\lm(g_2))}
   657	h'_2)g_2+ \\
   658	& \hspace{1cm} \sum_{i=3}^{s} (h_i+\frac{\lc(h_1g_2)x^{u_1}}{\lc(g_1)\lc(g_2)\lcm(\lm(g_1),\lm(g_2))}h'_i)g_i \\
   659	& = \sum_{i=1}^s \tilde{h}_i g_i,
   660	\end{align*}
   661	where $\tilde{h}_i$ is defined to be the polynomial multiplying $g_i$
   662	in the previous line.  By construction $\tilde{h}_1 > h_1$ and
   663	$\tilde{h}_i \geq h_i$ for all $i \geq 2$.  Write $x^{\tilde{u}_i}$
   664	for $\lm(\tilde{h}_i g_i)$.  Thus we have a new expression for $f$
   665	with either $\min(\val(\lc(\tilde{h}_ig_i))+ w \cdot \tilde{u}_i)$
   666	larger or this minimum the same and $d'$ smaller, which contradicts
   667	our assumptions on the respective maximality and minimality of these
   668	quantities.  We thus conclude that $f$ does not exist, so
   669	$\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I)) = \langle
   670	\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_1)),\dots,\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_s)) \rangle$ as
   671	required.
   672	\end{proof}
   673	
   674	Note that Algorithm~\ref{a:Buchberger} and the proof above also holds
   675	in the variation discussed in Remark~\ref{r:Zmodpm}.
   676	
   677	
   678	After applying Algorithm \ref{a:Buchberger} we have found a set
   679	$\{g_1,\dots,g_s\} \subset I$ such that $\{\inn_\prec(\inn_{w}(g_i)) :
   680	1 \leq i \leq s\}$ generates $\inn_\prec(\inn_{w}(I))$.  This means
   681	that $\{ \inn_w(g_i) : 1 \leq i \leq s \}$ is a (usual) Gr\"obner
   682	basis for $\inn_w(I)$ with respect to $\prec$, so in particular this
   683	set generates $\inn_w(I)$.  We thus conclude that the set
   684	$\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ is a Gr\"obner basis for $I$ with respect to $w$.
   685	
   686	
   687	This Gr\"obner theory shares many of the properties of standard
   688	Gr\"obner bases:
   689	
   690	\begin{enumerate}
   691	\item The Gr\"obner basis $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ generates $I$.  The
   692	  proof here is the standard one: if $f \in I$ then the normal form
   693	  $r$ of $f$ with respect to $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ lies in $I$, but
   694	  $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(r)) \not \in \inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I))$ unless
   695	  $r=0$.
   696	
   697	\item For any homogeneous ideal $I$, $w \in \mathbb R^n$, and monomial
   698	  term order $\prec$ there is a unique reduced Gr\"obner basis.  This
   699	  is a Gr\"obner basis $\{g_1,\dots,g_s \}$ with the property that the
   700	  $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_i))$ minimally generate

