   685	
   686	
   687	This Gr\"obner theory shares many of the properties of standard
   688	Gr\"obner bases:
   689	
   690	\begin{enumerate}
   691	\item The Gr\"obner basis $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ generates $I$.  The
   692	  proof here is the standard one: if $f \in I$ then the normal form
   693	  $r$ of $f$ with respect to $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ lies in $I$, but
   694	  $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(r)) \not \in \inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I))$ unless
   695	  $r=0$.
   696	
   697	\item For any homogeneous ideal $I$, $w \in \mathbb R^n$, and monomial
   698	  term order $\prec$ there is a unique reduced Gr\"obner basis.  This
   699	  is a Gr\"obner basis $\{g_1,\dots,g_s \}$ with the property that the
   700	  $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_i))$ minimally generate
   701	  $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I))$, and no monomial in $g_i$ except 
   702	  $\lm(g_i)$ is divisible by any
   703	  $\lm(g_j)$. This follows, as in the standard case,
   704	  from the existence of a strong normal form.  Specifically, if
   705	  $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I)) = \langle x^{u_1},\dots,x^{u_s} \rangle$,
   706	  then let $r_i$ be the remainder on dividing $x^{u_i}$ by any
   707	  Gr\"obner basis for $I$ with respect to $w$ and $\prec$.  Set
   708	  $g_i=x^{u_i}-r_i$.
   709	
   710	
   711	\item The Hilbert function of the two ideals $I$ and $\inn_w(I)$
   712	  (which live in different polynomial rings) agree.  While this
   713	  follows, as in the standard case, from the existence of a strong
   714	  normal form, there are other proofs; see, for
   715	  example,~\cite[Chapter 2]{SpeyerThesis} or \cite[Corollary 2.4.9]{TropicalBook}.
   716	\end{enumerate}
   717	
   718	\begin{remark} \label{r:homogeneity}
   719	We remark that the assumption that the ideal $I$, and the Gr\"obner
   720	basis $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$, are homogeneous is necessary for many of
   721	these properties of Gr\"obner bases.  For example, a set $\{
   722	g_1,\dots,g_s\} \subset I$ with $\inn_{w}(I) = \langle
   723	\inn_w(g_1),\dots,\inn_w(g_s) \rangle$ need not generate $I$ if it is
   724	not homogeneous.  A simple example is given by $I= \langle x \rangle
   725	\subseteq \mathbb Q[x]$ with the $2$-adic valuation: for $w=0$ the set
   726	$\{g_1=x+2x^2 \}$ satisfies $\inn_w(I)=\langle x \rangle = \langle
   727	\inn_w(g_1) \rangle$, but $\langle x \rangle \neq \langle x+2x^2
   728	\rangle$.
   729	\end{remark}
   730	
   731	This algorithmic approach to these initial ideals also allows a short
   732	computational proof of the following theorem of tropical geometry.
   733	See \cite{TropicalBook} for background definitions.
   734	
   735	\begin{corollary} \label{c:tropicaldoesnotdepend}
   736	 Let $K$ be a field with a valuation $\val$ for which there is a
   737	 homomorphism $\phi: \Gamma \rightarrow K^*$ with $\val(\phi(w))=w$.
   738	 Let $L$ be an extension field of $K$ with a valuation that restricts
   739	 to $\val$ on $K$.  Let $Y \subseteq (K^*)^n$, and let $Y_L = Y
   740	 \times_{\Spec(K)} \Spec(L)$.  Then $\trop(Y) = \trop(Y_L)$.
   741	\end{corollary}
   742	
   743	\begin{proof}
   744	Let $I \subset K[x_1^{\pm 1},\dots,x_n^{\pm 1}]$ be the ideal of $Y
   745	\subset (K^*)^n$.  Then the ideal of $Y_L$ is $I_L=I L[x_1^{\pm
   746	    1},\dots,x_n^{\pm 1}]$.  Let $J = I \cap K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$, and
   747	$J_L=I_L \cap L[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.  This intersection can be calculated
   748	by a (standard) Gr\"obner computation, so the ideals $J$ and $J_L$
   749	have the same generators: $J_L = J L[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.  The definition
   750	of the initial ideal of an ideal taking the valuation of the
   751	coefficients into account extends naturally to the Laurent polynomial
   752	ring.  By the fundamental theorem of tropical geometry (see, for
   753	example \cite[Theorem 3.2.3]{TropicalBook}) $w \in \mathbb R^n$ lies in
   754	$\trop(Y)$ if and only if $\inn_w(I) = \langle 1 \rangle$, and thus if
   755	and only if $\inn_w(J)$ contains a monomial.  Since $J$ and $J_L$ have
   756	the same generators, Algorithm~\ref{a:Buchberger} implies that
   757	regarding the elements of a Gr\"obner basis for $I$ with respect to
   758	$w$ as living in $L[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ gives a Gr\"obner basis for $I_L$
   759	with respect to $w$.  The residue field $\mathbb{L}$ of $L$ is an
   760	extension field of $\K$, so this means that $\inn_w(I_L) =
   761	\inn_w(I)\mathbb L[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.  An ideal contains a monomial if
   762	and only if the saturation by the product of all the variables is the
   763	unit ideal.  Since this can be decided by a (standard) Gr\"obner basis
   764	computation, this means that $\inn_w(I_L)$ contains a monomial if and
   765	only if $\inn_w(I)$ does.  
   766	This implies that $\trop(Y)=\trop(Y_L)$.
   767	\end{proof}
