   769	
   770	\section{Complexity}
   771	\label{s:complexity}
   772	
   773	Given a bound on the degrees of generators for $I$, it is useful to
   774	have a bound on the degrees of elements in a reduced Gr\"obner basis.
   775	The degree bounds in this context are the same as for usual Gr\"obner
   776	bases~\cite{MoraMoller}, \cite{Dube}, as we show below.  We also give
   777	a bound on the valuations of coefficients occurring in a reduced
   778	Gr\"obner basis when working over $\mathbb{Q}$ with the $p$-adic
   779	valuation.  For the degree bounds we use the formulation of
   780	Dub\'e~\cite{Dube}.
   781	
   782	
   783	\begin{theorem} \label{t:degreebound}
   784	Let $I = \langle f_1,\dots, f_l \rangle \subset K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ be a
   785	homogeneous ideal, with $\deg(f_i) \leq d$ for $1 \leq i \leq l$.  Fix
   786	$w \in \mathbb R^n$.  Then there is a Gr\"obner basis $\{g_1, \dots, g_s
   787	\}$ for $I$ with respect to $w$ with $\deg(g_i) \leq 2(d^2/2+d)^{2^{n-2}}$.
   788	\end{theorem}
   789	
   790	\begin{proof}
   791	In \cite{Dube} it is shown that if $\deg(f_i) \leq d$ for $ 1\leq i
   792	\leq l$, and $\{g'_1,\dots,g'_s\}$ is a standard homogeneous Gr\"obner
   793	basis with respect to some term order $\prec$, then the degree of each
   794	$g'_i$ is bounded by $2(d^2/2+d)^{2^{n-2}}$.  The proof given actually
   795	shows more: if $M$ is any monomial ideal whose Hilbert function agrees
   796	with that of $I$, then $M$ is generated in degrees at most
   797	$2(d^2/2+d)^{2^{n-2}}$.  Denote by $S_K$ the polynomial ring
   798	$K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ and by $S_{\K}$ the polynomial ring
   799	$\K[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.  By~\cite[Corollary 2.4.9]{TropicalBook} we have
   800	$\dim_{\K}(S_{\K}/\inn_w(I))_{\delta} = \dim_K (S_K/I)_{\delta}$ for all degrees
   801	$\delta$.  Since the initial ideal $\inn_w(I)$ is again a homogeneous
   802	ideal, all of its monomial initial ideals have the same Hilbert
   803	function, so we have
   804	$$\dim_{\K}(S_{\K}/\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I)))_{\delta} =
   805	\dim_{\K}(S_{\K}/\inn_w(I))_{\delta} = \dim_K (S_K/I)_{\delta}.$$ Let $M$ be the
   806	monomial ideal in $S_K$ with the same generators as
   807	$\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I)) \subset S_{\K}$.  As the Hilbert function of
   808	a monomial ideal does not depend on the coefficient field, $M$ has the
   809	same Hilbert function as $I$, so by \cite{Dube} $M$ is generated in
   810	degrees at most $2(d^2/2+d)^{2^{n-2}}$.  Choose homogeneous polynomials $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\} \subset
   811	I$ such that $\{\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_1)), \dots,
   812	\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_s)) \}$ is a minimal generating set for
   813	$\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I))$.  Then $\inn_w(I) = \langle \inn_w(g_1),
   814	\dots, \inn_w(g_s) \rangle$ so $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ is a Gr\"obner
   815	basis for $I$ with respect to $w$.  Since we have
   816	$\deg(\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(g_i))) \leq 2(d^2/2+d)^{2^{n-2}}$ by above, we
   817	deduce that $\{g_1,\dots,g_s \}$ is a Gr\"obner basis for $I$ with
   818	respect to $w$ with $\deg(g_i) \leq 2(d^2/2+d)^{2^{n-2}}$ for $1 \leq
   819	i \leq s$ as required.
   820	\end{proof}
   821	
   822	
   823	\begin{remark}
   824	Fix $w \in \mathbb R^n$, and let $J_w$ be the standard initial ideal of
   825	$I$ with respect to the weight vector $w$ (not taking the valuation
   826	into account).  There exists $v \in \mathbb R^n$ for which
   827	$\inn_{v-\ell w}(I)$ does not not depend on $\ell$ for $\ell > 0$.
   828	Such a $v$ can be chosen from any cell in the Gr\"obner complex of $I$
   829	that has $w$ in its recession cone; see \cite[Theorem
   830	  3.5.6]{TropicalBook}.  We then have 
   831	have $J_{w} = \inn_{v- w}(I)$; the minus sign is because the
   832	initial ideal taking the valuation into account uses min instead of
   833	max.  This means that any usual initial ideal, and thus any usual
   834	Gr\"obner basis, occurs in this setting, so any improvement to
   835	Theorem~\ref{t:degreebound} would also have to improve the bounds of
   836	\cite{MoraMoller} and \cite{Dube}.
   837	\end{remark}
   838	
   839	Since the valuations of coefficients also play an important role in
   840	computing these Gr\"obner bases, it is also useful to bound the
   841	valuations that may occur.  This is not possible in full generality,
   842	as the following example shows.
   843	
   844	
   845	\begin{example}
   846	
   847	\label{e:unbounded}
   848	Let $K=\mathbb{Q}(t)$ with the valuation of a rational function given
   849	by taking the lowest exponent occurring in a Taylor series for the
   850	function.  Fix an integer $a \gg 0$ and weight vector
   851	$w=(1,a,2a)$. Let $I$ be the ideal in $K[x,y,z]$ generated by the two
   852	polynomials $f=x+z$ and $g=x^2+(1+t^{a})xz+xy$.  We compute a
   853	Gr\"{o}bner basis by looking at the $S$-polynomial
   854	$S(f,g)=xf-g=-xy-t^{a}xz$. Computing the remainder on division by
   855	$\{f,g\}$ we obtain $yz+t^{a}z^2$ which is a nonzero polynomial with
   856	initial term $yz$.  It is added to the Gr\"{o}bner basis at this stage
   857	by the Buchberger Algorithm (Algorithm~\ref{a:Buchberger}).  Further
   858	running of this algorithm shows that $\{x+z, yz+t^az^2\}$ is a
   859	Gr\"obner basis for $I$.  This can also be seen from applying
   860	Buchberger's criterion (B1); see Section~\ref{s:Buchbergercriteria}.
   861	Notice that we started with polynomials where the valuations of all
   862	the coefficients were zero and we have an element of the reduced
   863	Gr\"{o}bner basis which has a coefficient with valuation $a$ showing
   864	that unbounded valuations may potentially occur when computing
   865	Gr\"{o}bner bases.  The field $K=\mathbb Q(t)$ is only chosen for
   866	concreteness; such an example exists for any nontrivially-valued
   867	field.
   868	\end{example}
   869	
   870	
   871	When $K=\mathbb{Q}$ with the $p$-adic valuation the valuation of
   872	coefficients that can occur in a reduced Gr\"{o}bner basis can be
   873	bounded in terms of the absolute values of the original coefficients.
   874	
   875	Let $I = \langle f_1,\dots,f_l \rangle$ be a homogeneous ideal in
   876	$\mathbb{Q}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with $\deg(f_i) \leq \delta$ for $1 \leq
   877	i \leq l$.  Fix $\val$ to be the $p$-adic valuation on $\mathbb Q$.
   878	Write $f_i = \sum c_{u,i}x^u$ where we assume (by clearing
   879	denominators or dividing by a common factor) that $c_{u,i} \in \mathbb
   880	Z$ and that for each $i$ we have $\min_u \val(c_{u,i}) = 0$.
   881	
   882	\begin{proposition}\label{proposition.complexity.valbound}
   883	Let $I=\langle f_1,\dots,f_l\rangle$ be a homogeneous ideal in
   884	$\mathbb{Q}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ with assumptions as above.  Let $C =
   885	\max_{u,i} |c_{u,i}|$.  Fix $w \in \mathbb R^n$.  Then there is a
   886	Gr\"obner basis $\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ for $I$ with respect to $w$, with
   887	$g_i = \sum_{u,i} b_{u,i} x^u$, that satisfies $$\val(b_{u,i}) \leq A/2
   888	\log_p (C^2 A),$$ where $A = \dim_{\mathbb Q}(I_D)$ for $D = 2(\delta^2/2+\delta)^{2^{n-2}}$.
   889	\end{proposition}
   890	
   891	\begin{proof}
   892	 As the
   893	 Hilbert functions of $I$ and $\inn_{w}(I)$ agree \cite[Corollary
   894	   2.4.9]{TropicalBook} we have that $\dim_{\mathbb Q} I_d =
   895	 \dim_{\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z} (\inn_{w}(I)_d)$ for all $d$.  Fix a term
   896	 order $\prec$ on $\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z[x_1,\dots,x_n]$.  Let $H(d) =
   897	 \dim_{\mathbb Q}(I_d)$.
   898	
   899	For $d\le D$, form an $H(d) \times\binom{n+d-1}{d}$ matrix $A_d$ with
   900	columns indexed by the monomials of degree $d$ ordered so that those
   901	in $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_{w}(I))_d$ come first.  The rows of $A_d$ are
   902	the coefficients of polynomials forming a $\mathbb Q$-basis for $I_d$;
   903	we may take these polynomials to be monomial multiples of the generators $f_i$, so
   904	all entries of $A_d$ have absolute value at most $C$.
   905	
   906	Let the submatrix of $A_d$ indexed by the first $H(d)$ columns be
   907	denoted by $M_d$.  Note that $M_d$ has full rank; if not, since $A_d$
   908	has rank $H(d)$, there would be a vector in the row-space of $A_d$
   909	with its first $H(d)$ entries zero, and thus there would be a non-zero
   910	polynomial $f$ in $I_d$ for which $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_{w}(f))$ does not
   911	lie in $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_{w}(I))$, which is a contradiction.
   912	
   913	Set $B_d=M_d^{-1}A_d$.  Note that the first $H(d)$ columns of $B_d$
   914	are an identity matrix, so the minor $\det((B_d)_J)$ of $B_d$ indexed
   915	by the set $J:=(\{1,\dots,H(d)\}\cup\{j\})\backslash \{i\}$ equals
   916	$(-1)^{H(d)-i}(B_d)_{ij}$.  Since $(B_d)_J=M_d^{-1}(A_d)_J$,
   917	\begin{displaymath}
   918	\begin{array}{rcl}
   919	  \val ((B_d)_{ij})&=&   \val (\det((B_d)_J)) \\
   920	  & = &\val(\det(M_d^{-1}(A_d)_J))\\
   921	  & = & -\val(\det(M_d))+\val(\det((A_d)_J)).
   922	\end{array}
   923	\end{displaymath}
   924	Hadamard's inequality (see for example \cite[Corollary
   925	  14.2.1]{Garling}) states that if $M$ is an $N \times N$ matrix with
   926	the absolute value of the entries bounded by $C$, then $|\det(M)| \leq
   927	C^N N^{N/2}$.  Thus $|\det((A_d)_J)| \leq C^{H(d)}H(d)^{H(d)/2}$.
   928	Since $\det(A_d)_J$ is an integer, $\val(\det((A_d)_J) \leq
   929	\log_p(|\det((A_d)_J)|)$.  By construction all entries of $M_d$ have
   930	nonnegative valuation, so $\val(\det(M_d)) \geq 0$.  Thus
   931	$$\val ((B_d)_{ij}))\le \log_p (C^{H(d)} H(d)^{H(d)/2}) = H(d)/2 \log_p (C^2H(d)).$$
   932	
   933	
   934	By Theorem~\ref{t:degreebound} there is a Gr\"obner basis
   935	$\{g_1,\dots,g_s\}$ for $I$ with respect to $w$ with $\deg(g_i) \leq
   936	D$, which can be chosen so $\{ \inn_{w}(g_1),\dots,\inn_w(g_s) \}$ is
   937	a Gr\"obner basis for $\inn_w(I)$ with respect to $\prec$.  The
   938	construction of the matrix $B_d$ guarantees that if $g_i$ has degree $d$
   939	then the coefficients of $g_i$ form a row of the matrix $B_d$.  This
   940	follows from the fact that there is a unique homogeneous polynomial
   941	$f$ in $I$ with $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_{w}(f))$ equal to a prescribed
   942	monomial $x^u$ with the property that the coefficient of $x^u$ in $f$
   943	is one, and no other term of $f$ lies in $\inn_{\prec}(\inn_w(I))$.
   944	Thus the valuation of the coefficients of $g_i$ is bounded as above.
   945	Since $H(d)$ is an increasing function of $d$, the bound is largest
   946	when $d=D$, so $H(d)=A$, from which we see that the valuations of any
   947	of the coefficients of any $g_i$ is bounded by $A/2 \log_p (C^2A)$ as
   948	required.
   949	\end{proof}
   950	
   951	
   952	
   953	
   954	\section{Implementation Issues}
   955	\label{s:implementation}
   956	
   957	We focus on  $K=\mathbb{Q}$ with the
   958	$p$-adic valuation.   This has been implemented as a
   959	package in \texttt{Macaulay2}~\cite{GrobnerValuations}.
   960	 As is common for Gr\"obner algorithms with coefficients in $\mathbb
   961	 Q$, a major issue in practical implementations is coefficient
   962	 blow-up.  We found examples where coefficients became so large that
   963	 computations would not terminate within the memory space limitations.
   964	 Thus it was necessary to consider ways to improve the speed and
   965	 efficiency of the algorithms, the two main ways of which are:
   966	
   967	\begin{enumerate}
   968	 \item Using criteria to decide a priori that certain $S$-polynomials reduce to zero;
   969	 \item Working over $\mathbb{Z}/p^m\mathbb{Z}$ for some suitably large $m\in\mathbb{N}$.
   970	\end{enumerate}
   971	
   972	\subsection{Choice of S-Polynomials - Buchberger's Criteria} \label{s:Buchbergercriteria}
   973	
   974	Suppose we are at some intermediate stage of the Buchberger Algorithm
   975	where we have a set $\mathcal P$ of critical pairs still to consider and we
   976	are about to compute the $S$-polynomial of the pair $(f_i,f_j)$.  Then
   977	
   978	\begin{description} 
   979	 \item[B1\label{B1}] holds if $\lcm(\lm(f_i),\lm(f_j))=\lm(f_i)\lm(f_j)$; 
   980	 \item[B2\label{B2}] holds if there exists some $k\ne i,j$ such that the pairs $(f_i,f_k)$ and $(f_j,f_k)$ are not in $\mathcal P$ and $\lm(f_k)$ divides $\lcm(\lm(f_i),\lm(f_j))$. 
   981	\end{description}
   982	
   983	
   984	From the work of Buchberger~\cite{Buchberger} for usual Gr\"obner
   985	bases, if either of these conditions hold then we know a priori that
   986	the $S$-polynomial reduces to zero. The proof can be found for example
   987	in~\cite{CLO}: the proof for \ref{B1} is Proposition 4, and the proof
   988	for \ref{B2} is Proposition 10 of \cite[\S 2.9]{CLO}.  The first proof
   989	follows through verbatim in this situation, while the second requires
   990	the same modifications as in the proof of
   991	Algorithm~\ref{a:Buchberger}.  We illustrate the usefulness of the
   992	criteria with an example.
   993	
   994	\begin{example} \label{e:BuchbergerCriterionExample}
   995	Let $K=\mathbb{Q}$ with the $2$-adic valuation and let $S$ be the
   996	polynomial ring $\mathbb{Q}[x_1,\dots,x_9]$. Let $I$ be the ideal
   997	generated by polynomials $\{- 3x_1x_4 + 6x_3x_4 + 3x_1x_5 + 92x_2x_5 +
   998	2x_3x_5 - 23x_2x_6 - 2x_3x_6, x_1x_8 + 7x_2x_8 - 4x_3x_8 - 6x_1x_9 -
   999	3x_2x_9, x_4x_8 + 3x_5x_8 - 3x_6x_8 - 24x_5x_9 - 3x_6x_9, - x_2x_4 -
  1000	4x_3x_4 + x_2x_5 + 4x_3x_5 + 23x_2x_6 + 2x_3x_6, - 13x_1x_7 - 4x_3x_7
  1001	+ 7x_2x_8 + 28x_3x_8 - 65x_1x_9 - 3x_2x_9 - 32x_3x_9, x_4x_7 +
  1002	27x_5x_7 - 9x_6x_8 + 5x_4x_9 + 135x_5x_9 - 9x_6x_9, - 4x_2x_5 -
  1003	16x_3x_5 + 3x_1x_6 + x_2x_6 - 2x_3x_6, 13x_2x_7 - 8x_3x_7 + x_2x_8 +
  1004	4x_3x_8 + 59x_2x_9 - 64x_3x_9, 8x_5x_7 + x_6x_7 - 3x_6x_8 + 40x_5x_9 +
  1005	5x_6x_9, 4x_2x_5x_8 + 16x_3x_5x_8 + 20x_2x_6x_8 - 10x_3x_6x_8 -
  1006	24x_2x_5x_9 - 96x_3x_5x_9 - 3x_2x_6x_9 - 12x_3x_6x_9\}$.  This is the
  1007	general fiber of a Mustafin variety in the sense of
  1008	\cite{MustafinVarieties}.  Its special fiber is the initial ideal with
  1009	respect to $w=0$.  
  1010	
  1011	At some intermediate step of the Buchberger Algorithm (Algorithm 
  1012	\ref{a:Buchberger}) we compute the normal form of the $S$-polynomial
  1013	$6x_3x_4x_6x_7 + 3x_1x_5 x_6x_7 + 24x_1x_4 x_5x_7+ 92x_2x_5x_6x_7 +
  1014	2x_3x_5x_6x_7 - 23x_2x_6^2 x_7 - 2x_3x_6^2 x_7 - 9x_1x_4x_6x_8 +
  1015	120x_1x_4x_5x_9 + 15x_1x_4x_6x_9$ of the polynomials $- 3x_1x_4 +
  1016	6x_3x_4 + 3x_1x_5 + 92x_2x_5 + 2x_3x_5 - 23x_2x_6 - 2x_3x_6$ and
  1017	$x_6x_7 +8x_5x_7 - 3x_6x_8 + 40x_5x_9 + 5x_6x_9$. Notice that the
  1018	condition B1 holds, so we know a priori that this $S$-polynomial will
  1019	reduce to zero, however when we try to compute the normal form, after
  1020	a few divisions we obtain a leading coefficient of $1.02624\dots
  1021	\times 10^{37,746}$ and after a few more divisions we have exceeded
  1022	the memory capabilities of the computer.
  1023	
  1024	 By implementing Buchberger's Criterion, the algorithm no longer considers this critical pair and we compute the Gr\"{o}bner basis to be 
  1025	$
  1026	\{ 3x_1x_4 - 6x_3x_4 - 3x_1x_5 - 92x_2x_5 - 2x_3x_5 + 23x_2x_6 + 2x_3x_6, 
  1027	x_1x_8 + 7x_2x_8 - 4x_3x_8 - 6x_1x_9 - 3x_2x_9, 
  1028	x_4x_8 + 3x_5x_8 - 3x_6x_8 - 24x_5x_9 - 3x_6x_9, 
  1029	x_2x_4 + 4x_3x_4 - x_2x_5 - 4x_3x_5 - 23x_2x_6 - 2x_3x_6, 
  1030	13x_1x_7 + 4x_3x_7 - 7x_2x_8 - 28x_3x_8 + 65x_1x_9 + 3x_2x_9 + 32x_3x_9, 
  1031	x_4x_7 + 27x_5x_7 - 9x_6x_8 + 5x_4x_9 + 135x_5x_9 - 9x_6x_9, 
  1032	- 4x_2x_5 - 16x_3x_5 + 3x_1x_6 + x_2x_6 - 2x_3x_6, 
  1033	13x_2x_7 - 8x_3x_7 + x_2x_8 + 4x_3x_8 + 59x_2x_9 - 64x_3x_9, 
  1034	8x_5x_7  - 3x_6x_8 + 40x_5x_9 + 5x_6x_9+ x_6x_7, 
  1035	 - 4x_2x_5x_8 - 16x_3x_5x_8 - 20x_2x_6x_8 + 10x_3x_6x_8 + 24x_2x_5x_9  + 3x_2x_6x_9 + 96x_3x_5x_9+ 12x_3x_6x_9  \}
  1036	$
  1037	\end{example}
