The paper changes the triage materially. Its native certificate route is Algorithm 2.1 (a terminating strong normal form) plus Algorithm 2.2/Buchberger pairs; Section 4 also gives the crucial easy case: Buchberger criterion B1 can bypass a reduction that otherwise produces a coefficient around \(10^{37{,}746}\) and exceeds memory. That rules out Track A for a planted basis—the paper explicitly supplies the algorithm—but leaves a plausible Track B family only if the witness exposes a short B1/S-polynomial identity while the routine reduction remains genuinely large. I’m checking whether that gap can be made nontrivial rather than merely asking the solver to copy the S-polynomial definition.
